    "created_at": "2015-09-04T18:13:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-04T18:13:35Z",
    "body": "I think I'm just not buying into the initial premise that Live HTML collections are bad (which are returned by the getElementsBy\\* methods), thus we should avoid making them available to new derived objects like ShadowRoot. If they were so bad, why was getElementsByClassName added so [relatively] recently? Yes, querySelectors and family are generalizations of these APIs, but there is something to be said for being able to optimize a specialized API in implementation, and frankly to have a choice of usage is also good IMO. Finally, there is no implementation burden. All these points have been made before as I read the above links.\n\nYet, not having read those discussions initially, and after reviewing the Shadow DOM and DOM specs, this seemed like a glaring omission. I'm certain other JavaScript authors will see it the same way and wonder, \"why not?\".\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
